<!--
  Deploy pipeline and rollback. Facts: packages/cli/commands/basecamp/deploy.md
  (the laptop path, preflight, docker save | ssh docker load, the -dirty mark),
  packages/cli/commands/deploy/index.md (BASECAMP_APP_ID hands fli deploy to
  basecamp:deploy), buildInitialSteps and rollback() in
  packages/basecamp/api/src/services/deployments/deployments.service.ts, the
  step runner in api/src/jobs/deployment-run.job.ts, and the swap and putBack
  in packages/outpost/src/docker.js. Step names are the strings
  buildInitialSteps writes, pinned by api/test/inline-app.test.ts.
  No zero-downtime claim: Outpost stops the live container before the new one
  starts, because a SQLite file takes one writer.
-->
<section id="deploy" class="bc-section">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Deploy pipeline</p>
    <h2>Ship with one command. Take it back with one button.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      A deploy becomes a release: built, loaded onto your machines, started, health-checked
      and written down. If the new container will not come up, the old one keeps serving.
    </p>

    <div class="bc-split">
      <ul class="bc-points">
        <li>
          <strong>Three ways to ship, one release.</strong>
          Run <code>fli deploy</code>, press Deploy on the App's page, or push to the branch
          it follows. Each is recorded with who shipped it, the commit and the image digest.
          From your laptop the image streams over ssh with <code>docker save | docker load</code>,
          so you need no registry.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Checked before anything is built.</strong>
          A required variable set nowhere, a database inside the container, a port the app
          does not listen on, or an env file baked into the image stops the release before the
          build. Whatever the App is missing is offered as one plan and applied when you say yes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A bad release leaves the good one serving.</strong>
          Outpost sets the live container aside, starts the new image by digest, and checks its
          health ten times, three seconds apart. If it never answers, the old one goes back.
          An app with a data volume gets a copy of it before the swap.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rollback ships the old release again.</strong>
          Roll back starts the image the previous release recorded, with the settings it shipped
          with, and rebuilds nothing. It takes an admin, and it does not rewind your data:
          restore the volume from the copy taken before the swap.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="bc-proof">
        <div class="flow" role="list">
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>fli deploy</strong> preflight, then docker build on your machine</span>
          <span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>docker save | ssh … docker load</strong> onto every machine the App is placed on</span>
          <span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>POST /deployments</strong> one release, named by the image digest the machine reports</span>
          <span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>Validate · Back up volume</strong> the data volume is copied before the swap</span>
          <span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>Start container</strong> Outpost sets the live one aside and starts the digest</span>
          <span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>Health check</strong> healthy, the old container is removed; otherwise it is put back</span>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          A laptop release of a container App with a data volume, from
          <code>packages/cli/commands/basecamp/deploy.md</code>. The step names are the ones
          <code>buildInitialSteps</code> in <code>deployments.service.ts</code> writes, and the
          ones the release page lists. A Deploy press on a git App runs Build image on the
          machine instead, and an image App runs Pull image. The swap and put-back are
          <code>packages/outpost/src/docker.js</code>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p class="bc-more">
      Read more:
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/cli/commands/basecamp/deploy.md">fli basecamp:deploy</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/basecamp/api/src/services/deployments/deployments.service.ts">releases and rollback</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/outpost/README.md">Outpost</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/basecamp/docs/hazards.md">the deploy hazards</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .flow { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; gap: .3rem; font-size: .9rem; }
  .flow .hop { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .1rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); border-radius: .75rem; padding: .5rem .9rem; background: var(--surface-raised); color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .flow .hop strong { color: var(--ink); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .flow .arrow { color: var(--ink-mute); text-align: center; line-height: 1; }
</style>
